BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (      # The Myth of the Two Wells) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (      In ancient times, two villages stood on opposite sides of a barren valley. Each possessed ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 168.0 Td (        a single well, but both ran shallow and bitter.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 158.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (      One day, a stranger appeared to the first village elder. "Pour your water into the valley,) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (        " the stranger said, "and it shall multiply threefold by the time it reaches your neighb) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 128.0 Td (        or.") Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 118.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 108.0 Td (      The elder feared deception. But remembering the old ways-that gifts given freely return as) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 98.0 Td (         blessings-she poured one jar into the valley. The water flowed downward, gathering stre) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 88.0 Td (        ngth from hidden springs, becoming three jars when it reached the second village.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 78.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 68.0 Td (      The second village's elder faced a choice: keep all the water, or honor the gift. Remember) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 58.0 Td (        ing that trust answered with trust weaves the strongest bonds, he sent back two jars.) Tj ET
